clients/rust (the Rust verifier client and the daisugi-gate and daisugi binaries)
is part of openDaisugi and is MIT licensed.

It builds on these crates from crates.io. Each is used under one of the
licences it offers:

  serde            MIT OR Apache-2.0
  serde_json       MIT OR Apache-2.0
  tree-sitter      MIT
  tree-sitter-bash MIT
  regex            MIT OR Apache-2.0
  thiserror        MIT OR Apache-2.0
  libc             MIT OR Apache-2.0
  jiter            MIT     (pydantic-core's JSON parser, for envelopes)
  z3-sys           MIT     (bindings to Z3)
  z3-src           MIT     (Z3 5.1.0's source, built by z3-sys)
  rustls           Apache-2.0 OR ISC OR MIT  (TLS for an llm_check and the
                   distiller's model call over https)
  rustls-native-certs Apache-2.0 OR ISC OR MIT (the system's root store)
  rusqlite         MIT     (daisugi's pathway store and trace journal;
                   Copyright (c) 2014-2021 The rusqlite developers)
  libsqlite3-sys   MIT     (the same authors; its build script compiles the
                   SQLite amalgamation it bundles, SQLite 3.45.0, into the
                   binary. SQLite itself is in the public domain:
                   https://sqlite.org/copyright.html)

rustls runs on these, each linked into daisugi-gate:

  ring             Apache-2.0 AND ISC (its crypto; carries C and assembly
                   code, compiled by its build script)
  rustls-webpki    ISC
  untrusted        ISC
  rustls-pki-types MIT OR Apache-2.0
  zeroize          Apache-2.0 OR MIT
  openssl-probe    MIT OR Apache-2.0 (finds the system's certificate files;
                   no OpenSSL code)
  subtle           BSD-3-Clause (constant-time compares; rustls needs it,
                   with no feature to leave it out)

The tests alone use rcgen (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and its own crates to make
a test CA; none of them is in a binary.

Z3 itself (Microsoft Research, MIT licence) is built from the source
z3-src pins and linked statically, with the C++ standard library of the
zig toolchain from the native prefix (LLVM libc++ and libc++abi:
Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception, which asks no notice for a binary). The
Rust standard library (MIT OR Apache-2.0, used under Apache-2.0,
Copyright the Rust Project Developers) is linked into every binary. tree-sitter and
tree-sitter-bash carry C code (the parser runtime and the generated bash
grammar), compiled into the binaries by their build scripts.

Transitive crates, each under one of the licences it offers: hashlink,
fallible-iterator, fallible-streaming-iterator, bitflags (for rusqlite),
ahash, bitvec, funty, hashbrown, indexmap, equivalent, itoa, lexical-parse-float,
lexical-parse-integer, lexical-util, num-bigint, num-integer, num-traits,
once_cell, radium, regex-automata, regex-syntax, aho-corasick, memchr,
smallvec, streaming-iterator, tap, wyz, zerocopy, zmij, getrandom,
cfg-if, serde_core, tree-sitter-language (MIT, Apache-2.0, Unlicense or
BSD-2-Clause), and the build-time crates cc, cmake, pkg-config, shlex,
autocfg, vcpkg, version_check, find-msvc-tools, proc-macro2, quote, syn,
unicode-ident (also Unicode-3.0), serde_derive, thiserror-impl. The exact
versions are in Cargo.lock.

Not linked, fetched on first use: daisugi's potion matcher downloads
minishlab/potion-base-8M (revision bf8b056651a2c21b8d2565580b8569da283cab23,
three files pinned by sha256 in src/pathways/potion/mod.rs) from Hugging
Face into $XDG_CACHE_HOME/opendaisugi/models. The model is MIT licensed
(its model card, https://huggingface.co/minishlab/potion-base-8M).

